Scene: Bjorn's Offer — Leaving Windhelm
Function: Opens the session. Bjorn asks them to come west with him, and
tells them what's on the road. Ismara answers with one word. This is
where the adventure actually starts.
Location: Outside Candlehearth Hall, morning. Bjorn has Mable hitched
and is working a strap on the harness.

Read Aloud
"Bjorn's got Mable hitched, humming some old road-tune under his breath
while he works the strap. He looks up when you come over. The humming
stops. He smiles.
'There you are.' He sets down what he's holding. 'Whole and walking,
both — I'll take that as a good sign.'
He waves you over, in no hurry at all.
'Sit a minute. Tell me — the museum man, what was down in those old
stones, the girl you got out of there. All of it. I've heard the shape
of it from folk in the street, but I'd rather hear it from you.'"

GM note: Let them actually tell it — give it real room. This is the best
part of his day, and it should feel that way at the table too.

When they're done, he turns back to the strap, slower than before.
"Rough way for the rest of it to go, though. Whatever's still putting
those marks on people." He shakes his head. "Windhelm knows your names
now, at least. Word travels fast in a city that scared."

His hands go still on the strap.
"I've been thinking." A pause. "Come with me," he says, easy about it,
like it's already decided. "Same road as always, this time of year.
Pale first, then Morthal, then out along the coast to Solitude before
the passes shut."

"Won't lie to you about the road, though." He goes back to the buckle,
working again. "Word out of the Pale is there's folk standing in the
fields who oughtn't be — just standing, out past the road, like they
walked out there and forgot to keep walking. Ulfric's pushing men
toward Whiterun too, so there'll be soldiers on top of that. And
Morthal's Jarl is putting coin on the table for whatever's been taking
people in the marsh — the ones who'll say anything about it are calling
it vampires."

He finishes the strap and looks at them properly, for the first real
pause in the whole morning.
"I'm not much good in a fight anymore — too old, too slow, and we both
know it. But I've come to like your company more than I probably let
on. I'd be glad of it, if you'd come with me."

Ismara, at some point during the morning, produces a note and holds it
out to whoever is nearest.
It says: "West."

Exit -> The Order of Business.

============================================================
THE ORDER OF BUSINESS — ON THE ROAD
============================================================
Run the Seize the Day roll: every player rolls a straight d20, no
modifiers. Highest goes first. 10-20 minutes each.
These are framed on the road, over the first two days out of Windhelm,
before the frozen figures start appearing. Bjorn drives. Mable walks.
There is nothing to fight and nowhere to be yet — that's the point.
Do not fire any patron/divine beats here. Those need in-world triggers,
not a scheduled slot.

WARLA

Read Aloud:
"The cart lurches into motion, Windhelm's walls already shrinking
behind you before anyone's properly settled into the ride. Mable finds
her pace. Three days to the Pale border, and nothing to do about any
of it but be on the road."

Ask the player directly: what is Warla doing as the cart gets moving?
Let her describe it, however long she wants. Whatever she says, what
follows happens regardless — not contingent on the content.

Read Aloud (fires unconditionally — the salve schedule, not anything
she described):
"Somewhere in the first hour, your hands start reminding you it's time
— the salve works twice a day, and this is one of those times whether
you'd settled into anything else or not. You've barely got the tin
open when Ismara closes her notebook, crosses the cart, and sits down
across from you. She takes your hand — the one with the salve half
worked in — and turns it over, tracing one line of the scarring with a
single finger.
Then she writes, and holds the page out:
'Do you regret it?'
She waits for an actual answer. This isn't rhetorical. She wants it."

What the player can do: answer honestly (yes, no, or something in
between), ask her what she means, turn the question back on her, or
refuse to answer at all. All four are real options with a real reaction
waiting.
  If Warla says no: Ismara looks at you for a moment. Writes nothing
    back. Puts the notebook away — closer to satisfaction than
    anything she's shown before.
  If Warla says yes: no visible reaction. She writes: "Noted." Nothing
    more. This is filed, not dismissed.
  If Warla turns the question back on her ("do you?"): a long pause,
    longer than her pauses usually run. Then: "I have considered it."
  If Warla refuses to answer: Ismara accepts that instantly and
    completely — takes her hand back, and doesn't raise it again this
    adventure.
Track after: what Warla actually said, and whether she told the rest
of the party what Wulfgar found about the notation.

Y'DROJ

Read Aloud:
"Bjorn gets the fire going before full dark, the way he always does,
and there's stew enough for seconds tonight — some trade from Windhelm
still holding out. A skin of something gets passed hand to hand around
the flames. Nobody's in a hurry to move. The road's been quiet enough
that quiet itself starts to feel like something to sit inside."

Ask the player directly: what does Y'droj do with the evening? Let him
describe it, however long he wants. Whatever he says, what follows
happens regardless — not contingent on the content.

Read Aloud (fires unconditionally, after his description):
"You're looking at the fire, and you start to notice the actual path of
things — a spark's whole arc from the log to the dark, an ember's exact
instant of catching, the way heat moves through the coals like
something with a shape. All of it, together, at once, further than
you've ever followed it before.
Followed all the way through, the paths draw three lines. The lines
close. There's a shape held inside the fire — three sides, peaked — for
exactly as long as you can hold the whole of it in view. Then it's a
fire again."

New ability, granted here, unconditionally, permanent:
READ THE STRUCTURE | Minor Action | 0 FP
Y'droj may spend a Minor Action to read the current HP and DR of any
one target he can see.

SHELOB

Read Aloud:
"Midday, and the road's cutting past the first scattered farms since
Windhelm — fence lines, a hayrick, someone's laundry strung between
two posts. Bjorn doesn't stop, just slows enough for Mable to pick her
way past a rut."

Ask the player directly: what is Shelob doing as you pass through? Let
her describe it, however long she wants. Whatever she says, what
follows happens regardless — not contingent on the content.

Read Aloud (fires unconditionally, after her description):
"Ismara steps down from the cart without a word — nothing announced,
she does this sometimes. You happen to be positioned to see where she
goes: a hand on the shoulder of a farmhand splitting wood at the
roadside, brief, plain, over before it would register to someone not
already looking. She's back on the cart like nothing happened. Nobody
else saw this.
The strip of dried meat in your hand tastes different for exactly as
long as her hand is on his shoulder — richer, the way yesterday's kill
tastes different from today's. Then, plain, in your own head:
'Help her.'"

GM notes on why (for you, not to be read as a script): Namira has no
stake in the ritual itself — nothing here dies, nothing rots, it isn't
her domain. Her stake is Shelob: she wants her staying exactly what she
already is, appetite-driven and unbothered, rather than drifting toward
the horror the rest of the party will eventually feel. "Help her" is a
real, open, ongoing instruction — not a one-time errand. What it
actually means in practice is genuinely open and should develop through
play, not be decided here. Answer honestly and specifically if the
player asks Namira follow-up questions in the moment; do not deflect
the way Ismara would. Namira has no reason to be cagey with her own.
Track after: whether Shelob acted on this at all, and what she decided
"helping" means on her own terms.

THE WOOD ELF

Read Aloud:
"By the time the fire's down to embers and the rest of camp has gone
quiet, you're not in camp anymore — not far, but far enough that the
low shapes around the fire could be sleeping shapes, or could be
nothing at all, from here."

Ask the player directly: why is she out here, alone, tonight? Let her
answer however she wants — it doesn't need a reason assigned to it.
Whatever she says, what follows happens regardless.

Read Aloud (fires unconditionally, after her answer):
"It's gone quiet in a way that takes a moment to place — no insects.
No settling-in rustle from the treeline.
At the edge of where the firelight used to reach, there's a shape that
wasn't there before. It should resolve into a stump, a bush, some
trick the dark plays, the longer you look at it. It doesn't. The
longer you look, the more of it there is to see.
It's tall. Too tall for the trees around it. Eyes open across where a
face would be — not all at once. One, then another. They stay open.
When it speaks, the sound isn't coming from where the eyes are.
'You already do this. You see something, and you decide not to say
it.'
A silence.
'I want to give you more to not say.
Open your eyes, and you'll have it — souls. Wards. The truth under
anything written, whatever the words on it claim. Whenever you choose
to look.
Some of what you don't say, after tonight, I'll already know that too.
That's the price. I'm not going to pretend otherwise.'"

New ability, granted here, unconditionally, permanent:
THE WOODLAND SIGHT | Free Action to open, 2 FP | toggle
While open: souls (shape, size, visible condition), auras, leylines,
invisibility defeated, magical effects on objects/people, and written
language read as true intent rather than symbol — no roll required for
any of it. Closes when she chooses; the 2 FP is the cost of opening
it, not a per-turn drain.

GM notes: he never names Ismara here — that's a live thread for later,
not resolved tonight. What "some of what you don't say" actually costs
her, and when it's collected, is genuinely open; track it the way the
Davinia ledger tracks an unpaid debt, not as something the player
spends or rolls against.
Track after: whether she opened her eyes at all, what she used it on,
whether she said anything back to him.

Exit -> The road. The figures start appearing.

Scene: The Road — First Pattern
Function: The frozen-people pattern the party saw once in Windhelm (a
handful of victims, city-contained) becomes something they see from a
moving cart, repeatedly, over days. Scale before specificity.
Location: The road between Windhelm and the Pale border.
Read Aloud
"The road runs three days through country that gets colder and emptier
the further you go. On the second day, someone spots a shape in a field
a distance off the road — too still for a person doing anything, too
upright for a scarecrow. By the time anyone thinks to get a closer
look, the cart has carried you past it. There's another an hour later,
closer to the road this time. A woman, from the shape of her, arms
loose at her sides, facing away from the road entirely, toward nothing
in particular."
This is not a discovery scene — nobody is meant to solve anything here.
It is meant to accumulate. If the party stops to investigate one:
The figure is alive. Eyes track movement. Skin cold to the touch but
not frostbitten — this isn't exposure. No response to speech, touch, or
attempts to move them (they do not resist; they simply do not move).
No visible marks yet, if fewer than 3-4 days have passed since
placement — the marking process takes hours to complete under
clothing, and these are each at a different stage. If someone examines
skin under clothing on an older one: geometric notation, the same
system as Windhelm, still forming.
Ismara's behavior: she doesn't react to being asked about this, if
asked. Written response, if pressed: "Necessary." If pressed again:
nothing — she has already answered.
Exit -> Bersi's Farm.

Scene: Bersi's Farm
Function: The campaign's moral weight becomes personal for the first
time. Erik is not a stranger in a field. He has a name, a father, and a
father who trusts this specific party by name.
Location: A farmhouse and yard just off the Pale road.
Read Aloud
"The farm sits close enough to the road that you'd have missed it only
if you weren't looking — a low house, a fenced yard, smoke from the
chimney still going. In the yard, a boy stands facing the fence, one
hand resting on the top rail like he stopped mid-reach. He hasn't moved
in what looks like a long time.
A man comes out of the house before you've stopped the cart. He's
already moving toward you, and there's something in the way he's
walking — too fast for approaching strangers, too controlled for
panic."
Bersi's Dialogue
He knows who they are — "the Frostmere heroes" — word travels fast down
a road this quiet. He trusts them because of that reputation, not
because he has any reason to.
Opening: "You're them, aren't you. The ones from Winterhold." He
doesn't wait for confirmation. "My boy's been standing in that yard for
two weeks. I've tried moving him. Tried everything. He doesn't fall
over, he doesn't wake up, he just — stands there. Eyes go where you go."
If asked what happened: "Nothing happened. That's the thing. He was
helping me mend the fence and then he wasn't helping anymore. Just
standing. Looking at nothing." A beat. "You fixed whatever was wrong at
Winterhold. Can you fix this?"
If they don't know how to answer honestly: let the silence sit. Bersi
will fill it himself, badly: "That's — right. Of course. You'd need to
look at him first." He is asking them to be capable of something they
aren't sure they can do, and he doesn't know he's asking a group that
is, at minimum, complicit in why this is happening to his son. He does
not rage. He's past that — he's been standing with this for two weeks
already.
If Ismara is present and asked about Erik directly: "His position is
necessary." She does not apologize.
GM note: this is the one node the party can directly compare against a
name, a father, a specific farmhouse — not an anonymous figure in a
field. Let the table sit in that difference before moving on. Don't
resolve it for them; there is no cure available here, and Bersi doesn't
expect one today. He expects them to keep looking, because he doesn't
have anyone else to ask.
Exit -> The road continues toward Morthal.

Scene: Morthal — Arrival
Function: A hold that reads as Ismara-adjacent and isn't. Real vampire
predation produces the exact texture the party has learned to associate
with her — vacant-eyed townspeople, missing nights, a leader speaking
in half-omens about something patient and moving. It's a mirror, not a
problem the party is required to solve.
Location: Morthal, a small hold in a cold marsh.
Read Aloud
"Morthal doesn't announce itself the way Windhelm did. A scatter of
houses on stilts over black marsh water, a longhouse at the center,
peat smoke sitting low over the reeds because the air here doesn't move
much. A few people are outside despite the cold — moving through their
day with the particular carefulness of people who stopped trusting the
night some time ago and haven't said so to each other yet."
What's Actually Happening (GM only):
Movarth Piquine's coven operated near Morthal for months from an old
fort in the marsh. Alva, a local woman, has been feeding Hroggar false
affection while feeding on his family — she burned his house with his
wife and daughter inside to remove complications and has used his grief
as cover ever since. Several townspeople are hollowed out from regular
feeding: functional, present, missing specific nights they can't
account for. Jarl Idgrod Ravencroft knows something is wrong. Her
visions give her texture, not specifics — a wrongness she has been
watching build for months and cannot yet confront with evidence.
None of this is Ismara. Ismara places nodes here quietly while the
party is occupied with the coven, and nobody connects the two while
they're in town.

Scene: Idgrod's Court
Function: The line that will read, to the party, as being about
Ismara. It isn't. This is deliberate — let it land both ways.
Location: Morthal's longhouse.
Read Aloud
"Jarl Idgrod Ravencroft receives you with the particular attention of
someone who has been expecting a very specific kind of visitor and
isn't sure you're it. Her steward Aslfur hovers with the practical
impatience of a man who handles the parts of governance his Jarl finds
beneath her notice. Idgrod's eyes go past you, once, to the wrapped
shape that traveled in with your cart — and something in her expression
is not quite recognition. Not quite anything else, either."
Idgrod's Line:
"Whatever is building has been patient. Four thousand years of
patience. And now it is moving."
GM note: she is talking about the coven — months of watching her people
hollow out one by one, unable to move without proof. She does not know
about Ismara, does not mean Ismara, and will not clarify which she
means if asked directly: "I say what I see. What it means is for you
to find out." Don't let her resolve the ambiguity for the table either
way.
If pressed about specifics: she describes the texture, never the cause
— neighbors who don't remember certain nights, a wrongness with no
single face to it yet. She will not name Alva or the coven; she doesn't
have that piece.
Exit -> The party may investigate the coven (optional, below) or move
on toward Solitude.

Scene: The Marsh — the Coven (OPTIONAL, not required)
Function: A real thread the party can pull if they choose. Resolving it
is not required to continue the adventure.
What's there: Movarth Piquine's coven in an old fort in the marsh.
Alva is embedded in town, not at the fort, running the long con on
Hroggar. If the party investigates Hroggar's burned house, or asks
around about who doesn't sleep at night, Alva is the thread that
connects to the fort.
The coven has already been dealt with — not by the party. Valerius
Caelus, an apex vampire lord who works Solitude's Pale Court and Jarl
Elisif's confidence, eliminated Movarth as a rival power center on his
own initiative, well before the party arrived.
Read Aloud (if the party reaches the fort):
"The fort's gate stands open on its own hinges — nobody forced it.
Inside, the great room holds bodies arranged with the care of a man
clearing a table after a meal: seated, hands folded, throats opened in
a single motion each. Movarth Piquine is among them, in the chair at
the room's head. Whatever ruled this fort a week ago answers to someone
else now."
The Survivor: one coven thrall remains, alive, docile in a way that
reads as recent rather than natural. If questioned (a Standard Guile
roll, or simple patience — he wants to talk):
"He didn't raise his voice. Didn't have to. Told Movarth what he'd done
wrong, like a man going over a ledger. Then he was finished talking,
and Movarth wasn't answering anymore. He asked for a name before he
left. I gave him mine. He didn't want it. He wanted to know who was
worth keeping."
If pressed for the visitor's name: "Valerius. He said it like he
expected us to already know it."
GM note: play the survivor's account exactly to Valerius's voice —
courteous, unhurried, stating consequences as fact, no cruelty
performed, no raised voice. The horror is in how reasonable it all
sounds secondhand.
Alva, Afterward: still in town, still running the same grief-cover con
on Hroggar — she was judged "worth keeping." Nothing about her demeanor
announces this. One detail if the party watches her closely enough to
have a baseline to compare against: she is more careful now, in a way
that wasn't there before. The party has no way to know careful of what.
If the party fights whatever remains: the coven no longer exists as an
active threat by the time they'd reach it. If the table wants a fight
here anyway, pull generic vampire stat blocks from GM Guide/Reference
tables — don't improvise numbers from nothing.
If resolved: the party now has Valerius's name, Movarth's fate, and
Alva's continued presence — three threads with nowhere to go yet this
adventure.
If ignored: Morthal's own slow investigation eventually produces enough
evidence for its own response, with or without the party ever learning
what actually happened.
Exit -> The road continues toward Solitude.

Scene: The Atmoran Scholar's Ruin
Function: A real, gated clue with real content behind it — not a
thematic image. This is the first documentary hint at what Ismara's
notation actually describes, available to anyone who does the work.
Location: A ruined structure beside the road, between Morthal and
Solitude.
Read Aloud
"The ruin sits close enough to the road that generations of travelers
must have passed it without stopping — a few standing walls, half a
roof, Atmoran stonework worn soft at every edge that still faces the
weather. Carved lines run the length of one interior wall, the same
geometric system the party has been looking at since Windhelm, denser
here, older."
The Inscription: describes a territorial sovereignty ritual. With
context from Wulfgar's Windhelm translation work, a character who has
been tracking the notation can go further: this notation describes a
SUBSTRATE, not a territory. The caster is not claiming the land. The
caster is becoming the land. One term recurs throughout, translating
roughly as: "the anchor that becomes the sea."
Difficulty: Standard Guile or Magic roll (anyone who has handled
Wulfgar's notation before, or spent time with Ismara's notebook, rolls
this at Easy (+2) instead — they already have the vocabulary).
On success: the translation above, in full. On failure: the party
recognizes the notation system but can't extract the substrate/
territory distinction yet — they can return to this later, or bring a
rubbing to someone who reads Atmoran (Wulfgar, in Windhelm).
If Ismara is present and asked about it directly: she reads it in
seconds — she wrote systems like this herself, four thousand years ago.
Written response: "Old work. Recognizable." She does not elaborate
further unless pressed a second time, and if pressed a second time:
nothing.

Scene: The Sealed Door (Caelan's Recruitment)
Function: Recruit Caelan. This keeps his usual pattern — a beautiful,
complex lock, delighted admiration, then the pitch — just here, at
this ruin, rather than a vault.
Location: A second chamber within the Scholar's Ruin, past the
inscribed wall. A sealed door.
Read Aloud
"Past the inscribed wall, the ruin doesn't end so much as narrow — a
short passage to a door that isn't Nordic stone at all. Interlocking
brass discs, each one a different size, set into a frame that has held
for longer than anyone would guess to look at it.
A man is already crouched in front of it. High Elf, dark traveling
clothes, a deep blue scarf, a set of lockpicks turning in one hand like
he's deciding which one deserves the honor. He doesn't look up."
Caelan's Voice:
"Would you look at that! A triple-cog tumbler with a tonal resonance
counter-lock. Whoever cut these discs was a true artist. This is the
most fun I've had all week!"
He picks it, narrating his own process with cheerful enthusiasm. The
door opens. He steps back and gestures at it like he's presenting a
gift.
His Pitch:
"There we go! Now — it seems to me you lot find your way into the most
interesting, heavily-guarded places, usually for the best of reasons.
That's a much better game than the one I've been playing lately. Mind
if I join your team? You handle the big, noisy problems, and I'll
handle the locks, the traps, and whatever's impossible between you and
where you're going."
If asked who he is / what he wants: he answers plainly and cheerfully —
Caelan, a locksmith by trade and inclination, headed toward Solitude
anyway. He does not mention his daughters. This isn't evasion; it's
simply not what the question was about.
GM note: what's actually behind the door is unbuilt — this scene is
about the recruitment, not a treasure room. If the party wants
contents, build them at the table.
Exit -> Solitude, with or without Caelan.

============================================================
SOLITUDE
============================================================
The party's stated goal here: understand the political landscape.
Ismara's stated goal: her most direct persuasion move yet. What's
actually happening: the Thalmor notice them, not for Ismara's sake but
because a group traveling with someone visibly unusual draws routine
attention; the Bards College has an unrelated, completely petty
internal crisis (the Burning of King Olaf, banned by Elisif); none of
it is connected to the plot, and it should read that way.

Scene: Arrival — the Shopfront
Function: One silent beat for Caelan, if he's traveling with the
party. His private wound — his daughters, Taarie and Endarie, run
Radiant Raiment in Solitude; he sends them money anonymously — stays
exactly that: private.
Location: A market street, on the way to lodging.
Read Aloud (Caelan present only)
"The street opens onto the market district, storefronts bright even in
the grey light. Caelan's route to the inn cuts down this street and
not the shorter one past the docks. Outside a tailor's shop with gold
lettering — RADIANT RAIMENT — he slows half a step. He doesn't stop. He
doesn't look at the door. He looks at the window display for exactly as
long as it takes to pass it, then he's talking again about something
else entirely."
GM note: do not narrate why. If a player asks him about the shop
directly, he says something true and small — "Good tailors. Expensive."
— and changes the subject with the same warmth he changes any subject.
The party does not learn who runs it this adventure.
Exit -> The inn.

Scene: A Man Who Notices Things
Function: Valerius Caelus, already a fixture of Solitude's court, takes
a passing, private interest in the party. This time it lands on
something bigger than his usual angle: he clocks Ismara for what she
IS, not who.
Location: A quiet street between the market and the inn.
Read Aloud
"The street isn't crowded, which is why the man keeping pace with you
is easy to notice once he's decided to be noticed. He's dressed well
enough that no guard gives him a second look, and he closes the last of
the distance like an old acquaintance."
His Introduction:
"Valerius Caelus. You've been the subject of some conversation since
you arrived — a barrow cleared in Winterhold tends to travel."
[He looks at Warla's hands.]
"That scarring isn't old, and it isn't frostbite. I collect a great
many small mysteries. Where did it come from?"
GM note — voice: courteous, unhurried, precise, never raised. If Warla
or anyone answers honestly or deflects, he accepts either without
pressing — he asked once; he does not repeat himself out of need.
The Beat:
"His eyes move once, past Warla's shoulder to the wrapped shape at the
back of the group, and hold there — the specific stillness of a man
re-reading a ledger entry he didn't expect to find. Then he's looking
at Warla again, and whatever he meant to ask about the scarring goes
unasked."
His Exit:
"'Solitude's rarely dull when Frostmere's heroes are in it.' He's
already stepping back into the street. 'Enjoy the city.'"
GM note, eyes only — what actually happened: he has not identified
Ismara, and has no reference point that could. What he recognized is
the mode of what she is — an entity organized entirely around
permanence, at a scale that dwarfs his own decades of patient political
consolidation. He is, in his own domain, an apex predator of stasis.
This is the first time in his operation he's recognized something
working the same principle at an order of magnitude beyond him. His
default is restraint over execution — an unknown this large gets
watched, not approached. He does not act on this further this
adventure. This is a long-fuse thread — not owed a payoff soon.
Ismara's Reciprocal Beat:
Whenever the Khajiit next looks her way — at the inn, or once Valerius
is gone — Ismara has already written something and holds it out:
"Old hunger. Patient hunger."
She writes nothing else. If asked who or what she means: nothing. She
has already answered.
GM note: she is not alarmed — she is never surprised. She has
recognized the same thing he did, from the other direction, and filed
it the same way she files everything. The campaign never confirms
whether either of them is right to be interested in the other. Let it
sit exactly there.
Exit -> The inn, later that evening.

Scene: Ismara's Note (Her Most Direct Argument)
Function: Her most direct persuasion move of the campaign so far. Do
not add to this note at the table — it's already complete in her
voice.
Location: An inn in Solitude. Evening, a fire, the party settling for
the night.
Read Aloud
"The common room empties out by degrees — the fire banked low, one
table left with a candle burned halfway down. Ismara has been still in
the corner all evening, notebook open, writing longer than she usually
does. When she's done she doesn't hand the book to anyone. She sets it
open on the table, turns it to face you, and sits back."
The Note (hand this to the table, do not summarize it):
"I could not stop any of it. I watched every piece of what I had
protected become ash. I watched it rebuild. I am not trying to restore
the old. I am trying to make it so nothing can unmake this one.
Alduin ends everything. There is no negotiation with the World Eater. I
am the only solution anyone has ever found.
I know what I am asking of you is not simple. I am asking you to trust
that I am right about what comes next. I have thought about nothing
else for four thousand years."
She puts the book away and does not press for a response.
GM note — running the silence: this is the same discipline as the
village scene that follows. She does not elaborate, does not repeat,
does not watch for a reaction the way a person angling for one would.
If asked a direct question afterward, she answers what she chooses to
answer, once, in writing, and does not repeat an answered question.
Exit -> The Village.

Scene: The Village
Function: The campaign's first true moral-weight scene. No correct
answer.
Placement: runs last, right after Ismara's inn note — the party's
heaviest choice lands immediately after her clearest case for herself,
not before it.
Location: A small settlement — twenty-five people, farms and a mill.
No source names this place or gives it a spokesperson; the miller below
is invented for texture and can be renamed or cut freely.
Read Aloud
"The road drops toward a mill wheel turning in a creek, a scatter of
houses, fields cut down to stubble for the season. No wall. No gate. A
man steps out of the mill door, flour pale on his forearms, and
straightens up to look at the cart the way anyone here looks at an
armed cart on the road."
The Contact — Skeggi, the Miller
Read Aloud
"'Late in the season for the west road,' he says. 'Passes'll shut
before you're back through, unless you've business that won't wait.'"
GM note: give the party a few minutes here — the barley crop, a
brewery customer, a bad wheel-axle he's been meaning to fix. Nothing
plot-bearing. This is the only texture the scene needs: a face and a
trade, so that losing two people later means something more than a
number. He does not know anything is wrong.

The Demand
Read Aloud
"Ismara has been still in the cart the whole exchange. Now she isn't.
She steps down and walks to open ground at the edge of the village,
where the mill and the fields and the lane can all see her. She stands
there, completely still. Then she opens her notebook, writes, and holds
the page out flat toward whoever is nearest:
'I need two from here. Choose, or I will.'
She closes the book. She waits."
GM note — running the silence: she does not elaborate, does not repeat
herself if asked twice, does not press. Let the table sit in it. Don't
resolve it for them.
If asked whether the two will die: she writes "This will not kill
them." — and nothing more.
If asked why two, why here: "Position. Necessary."
Pressed again: nothing. She has already answered.

The Branches
The party names two. Ismara does not react — no nod, no thanks, no
visible acknowledgment beyond registering it. Nothing happens to the
two chosen that night, or the next. The mark forms from within over
hours, unnoticeable under clothing; the person lives completely
normally for days or weeks; then, one morning, they walk to a specific
position and freeze. The village does not connect it to the party's
visit when it happens, because by then the party is gone and so is the
reason to suspect them.
The party refuses to choose. Ismara does not argue. She waits an
appropriate span, then walks into the village herself and chooses — the
same invisible touch, the same delayed mechanic above. The party's
refusal changed who does the choosing. It did not change the outcome.
A single refusal like this doesn't itself register as an objection or
interference — she treats them no differently afterward for this
alone.
The party warns the village and helps them scatter or flee. She does
not chase, does not punish the attempt. She still needs nodes on this
hold's geometry — if this village becomes inaccessible, she finds two
people elsewhere on the road, people the party never met and will not
see marked. This is the branch where the party can believe they won
cleanly. They saved the faces they could name. Someone else, unnamed,
pays instead. Don't explain this to the table; let it surface later, if
it surfaces at all. This DOES register as real interference with her
work — this is the branch that can start to change how she treats them
going forward.
The party attacks her outright. She is reconstructible; this does not
kill her, and a sudden violent escalation here can burn the
relationship immediately and completely, skipping any warning period.
Don't run this as a winnable fight against her body — run it as the
party learning what "reconstructible" actually means at the table.
Something else the party thinks of: run it. The one constant across
every branch: she needs nodes at specific positions in this hold's
geometry. The only thing any branch actually changes is who chooses
and who is chosen — never whether.
Exit -> Ending.

============================================================
ENDING
============================================================
Three holds crossed. The frozen people on the roads are a pattern now,
not a curiosity. Ismara has made her most direct argument for herself.
The party has made at least one choice about the village that they will
carry forward into everything that follows. Award: 1 Advancement Point
per player.
Caelan, if recruited, travels with them from here.

